# What may never be rsynced to the server.
#
# Passed to every rsync in deploy/deploy.sh with --exclude-from, and to the identity-service
# rsync in deploy/README.md. It lives in deploy/ rather than the repository root because
# deploying is the only thing that reads it, and a root dotfile invites being edited by
# somebody solving an unrelated problem.
#
# Two incidents on this box, both BidZA's and both cheap to inherit:
#
#   A developer's .env.local was synced to production and baked a localhost URL into the client
#   bundle, while --delete removed the server's own .env.production in the same pass. The pattern
#   was .env at the time. It is every env file now, and it must not be narrowed again: the server's
#   configuration is the server's, and ours has no business overwriting or deleting it.
.env
.env.*
*.env

# The proposal is confidential and no deployment or publication is authorised. It has no reason to
# be anywhere near a web root, and the only reliable way to keep it out is to never sync it.
Documentation/

# Anything shaped like a credential. None of these belong in a build output; if one appears there,
# the exclusion is the second line of defence and the scan in deploy.sh is the first.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.keystore
*.jks
id_rsa*
*.sqlite
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# Build and tool state. dist/ is what we publish, so it is not excluded here — the exclusions are
# relative to whatever directory is being synced, and we sync dist/ itself.
node_modules/
.git/
.github/
.DS_Store
*.log
*.tsbuildinfo
coverage/
test-results/
playwright-report/
.gradle/
DerivedData/
xcuserdata/
